body:
A caller that constructs HiveCatalog directly has to call setConf before initialize. Nothing enforces that order, and skipping it does not fail:
HiveCatalog catalog = new HiveCatalog();
catalog.initialize("hive", properties);
initialize finds no configuration, logs "No Hadoop Configuration was set, using the default environment Configuration" and carries on against the ambient environment. Whatever the caller had prepared, the HMS thrift URIs, kerberos and SSL settings, is quietly gone, and the problem only shows up later when the catalog talks to the metastore.
Getting it right today takes three lines in a fixed order:
HiveCatalog catalog = new HiveCatalog();
catalog.setConf(configuration);
catalog.initialize("hive", properties);
HadoopCatalog already avoids this with a convenience constructor. After this change HiveCatalog matches it:
HiveCatalog catalog = new HiveCatalog(configuration, properties);
What changes
The new constructor takes a property map rather than a single warehouse string, because HiveCatalog reads more than one key:
public HiveCatalog(Configuration conf, Map<String, String> properties) {
  setConf(conf);
  initialize("hive", properties);
}
The Javadoc on setConf and initialize now points at it. Closes #16134.
Testing
HiveCatalogConstructorDemo, whose source is below, builds a Configuration with one prepared setting (SASL enabled), plus a metastore URI and a warehouse location. It creates a catalog the old way with setConf skipped, then through the new constructor, and prints what each catalog holds. It runs against the real HiveCatalog on the module's test classpath, and no metastore is needed because construction does not connect.
Setup, on JDK 21, with an init script that prints the module's test classpath:
allprojects {
  if (path == ':iceberg-hive-metastore') {
    afterEvaluate {
      tasks.register('printTestClasspath') {
        dependsOn tasks.named('testClasses')
        dependsOn sourceSets.test.runtimeClasspath
        doLast {
          println 'CLASSPATH=' + sourceSets.test.runtimeClasspath.asPath
        }
      }
    }
  }
}
$ ./gradlew --console=plain -q -I print-classpath.gradle :iceberg-hive-metastore:printTestClasspath > classpath.out
$ CP=$(sed -n 's/^CLASSPATH=//p' classpath.out)
After, with HiveCatalog from this branch:
$ javac -proc:none -cp "$CP" -d out HiveCatalogConstructorDemo.java
$ java -cp "out:$CP" HiveCatalogConstructorDemo
[main] WARN org.apache.iceberg.hive.HiveCatalog - No Hadoop Configuration was set, using the default environment Configuration
setConf skipped: sasl=null, uris=thrift://metastore.example:9083, warehouse=/warehouse
new constructor: sasl=true, uris=thrift://metastore.example:9083, warehouse=/warehouse
Before, with HiveCatalog taken from upstream/main:
$ git checkout upstream/main -- hive-metastore/src/main/java/org/apache/iceberg/hive/HiveCatalog.java
$ ./gradlew --console=plain -q :iceberg-hive-metastore:compileJava
$ javac -proc:none -cp "$CP" -d out HiveCatalogConstructorDemo.java
HiveCatalogConstructorDemo.java:21: error: constructor HiveCatalog in class HiveCatalog cannot be applied to given types;
    HiveCatalog catalog = new HiveCatalog(prepared, properties);
                          ^
  required: no arguments
  found:    Configuration,Map<String,String>
  reason: actual and formal argument lists differ in length
1 error
The first run shows the problem. With setConf skipped the catalog warns, loses the prepared SASL setting and keeps only what the property map carried. The new constructor keeps both.
Reproducer source: HiveCatalogConstructorDemo.java
